-- Insert article for 'Jantar Mantar to Golgotha'
INSERT INTO articles (id, title, author, job_title, works_at, category, image_url, alt_text, content, excerpt, publish_date, read_time, is_featured, language) VALUES
('jantar-mantar-to-golgotha', 'Jantar Mantar to Golgotha', 'Renji Sam', 'Writer', 'True Bread Media', 'Faith', 'assets/images/articles/jantar-mantar-to-golgotha.jpg', 'Reflection on justice, youth anxiety, and the hope of Christ from Jantar Mantar to Golgotha', 'There is something deeply unsettling about the times we live in. On one side, we watched education reformer Sonam Wangchuk choose hunger over hatred. His hunger strike became more than a protest—it became a reminder that sometimes the loudest message is spoken by an empty plate. Whether people agreed with every demand or not, the image was unforgettable: one individual willing to endure personal suffering to draw attention to what he believed was a larger public concern.

On the other side, social media has been flooded with allegations, discussions, and viral claims surrounding the NEET 2026 examination, including videos by Shashikant Sathur that claim to expose serious irregularities. Some of these claims remain disputed or unverified, while others have intensified public demands for greater transparency. Regardless of where the facts ultimately lead, one truth is undeniable: millions of students and parents are anxious. That anxiety deserves attention.

The Real Question Is Not Just “Was the Exam Fair?”
The bigger question is: What are we teaching our children when trust becomes optional?

Children spend years sacrificing birthdays, sleep, hobbies, friendships, and often their mental health for a single examination. Parents mortgage savings, invest in coaching, and build their family''s future around one dream. If young people begin to believe that honesty matters less than influence, preparation matters less than privilege, or effort matters less than loopholes, we lose far more than an examination. We lose faith.

A Generation Carrying Invisible Weight

Today''s students are not simply preparing for competitive exams. They are carrying:
* Fear of failure.
* Fear of disappointing their families.
* Fear of being left behind.
* Fear that the system itself may not reward merit fairly.

Many already struggle with anxiety, depression, burnout, and loneliness long before they enter adulthood. Every controversy surrounding public examinations adds another layer of emotional burden.

Parents: Your Child Is Watching More Than the News

Children observe how adults react. Do we teach them outrage without evidence? Do we encourage critical thinking? Do we remind them that allegations deserve careful investigation, due process, and transparency rather than instant conclusions?

Parents cannot control national systems. But they can control the emotional climate at home. A child who believes they are loved beyond their rank is stronger than a child who believes their worth depends on a scorecard.

Education was never meant to produce survivors. Education should produce thinkers, compassionate citizens, ethical professionals—people who will one day improve the very systems they inherit.

Jesus Christ and his lived principles and values entice human minds still. He lived 33 years in this world when corruption, mistreatments, capitalism, racism, discrimination and all social injustice was at its fullest. He chose to live a life that was seen by others with kindness and compassion, and he made sure the sufferings and pain became visible and cared for.

Salvation becomes one of the most important topics of attention at this moment. Lord himself came to this world, suffered for his people and died for them in the cross so that no man''s life gets perished by sin (John 3:16). His life entitles sonship (Romans 8:14-17) which was lost for mankind. His sacrifices hold close to the reconciliation which was done for mankind by one man. His crucifixion portrays God''s agape love (Romans 5:8) which was hindered but restored by Jesus.

Can one person''s action change the future of all generation?
Yes, indeed. With the life of Jesus, one gets to see that the unwavering love of God Almighty brings in hope and transference in everyone who believes.

Golgotha is a place where man is considered beyond his gender, race, creed, and existence. Even if Golgotha (Matthew 27:33) means “place of skull”, Jesus and His mighty sacrifice rename it as a place of life regeneration. It is a place where love unites with justice, unfailing effort meets transformation, and dismantling toxicity meets a hypocritic system. Definitely blood is shed, but victory and resurrection are the sweet fruit that awaits generations yet to come.

History is repeating; a generation will rise with faith and trust which is not given to them by just words of passing government but something they earned when the truth set them free (John 8:32) of slavery of corruption, unjust privileges and biased statistics.

Because they will experience Jesus who spoke for the people, stood beside broken ones and poured out his life for his beloved ones without asking. Sonam Wangchuk being a mere human with downfalls went to the extent of 23 days of hunger strike, our Lord gave died so that no one must go through sin, curse and strings of death again. He revolutionized the system when no one gave glance at the underprivileged and exploited ones in the society. He strived for us to live in freedom, fairness and peace.',

 'An August 2026 reflection connecting educational justice concerns, youth anxiety, and Christ''s redemptive love—calling a generation toward truth, fairness, and hope.', '2026-08-01', 10, TRUE, 'en');


